  "caption": "98% fail this FAANG SQL interview trap 💀\n\nOption A vs Option B — both LOOK identical. One of them silently returns ZERO rows because of a single NULL sitting in the subquery.\n\nDrop your vote (A or B) in the comments before you scroll 👇 Full breakdown + the free simulator link is pinned below.\n\n#SQL #DataAnalyst #FAANGInterview #SQLTips #Manodemy #LearnSQL #TechInterview #DataAnalytics",
  "pinnedAnswer": "PINNED ANSWER — Day 04 (NOT IN + NULL trap) 💡\n\nOption A is the trap! ❌\nNOT IN treats the subquery's result set literally — if even ONE row in (SELECT dept_id FROM departments) is NULL, the entire NOT IN comparison evaluates to UNKNOWN for every single row, causing the outer query to return ZERO rows silently (no error thrown!).\n\nOption B fixes this with `WHERE dept_id IS NOT NULL` before the NOT IN runs, making the comparison 100% safe.\n\nRule of thumb: NEVER use NOT IN against a subquery that can contain NULLs. Use NOT EXISTS instead — it doesn't have this failure mode.\n\nTry it yourself free → link below.",
